WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.385
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.685 --> 00:00:08.909
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:16.354
Unit twenty-five: Future Perfect Continuous. Today you will show how long an activity will have lasted by a future point.

4
00:00:16.654 --> 00:00:19.330
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:23.743
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.583 --> 00:00:25.584
Will have been.

7
00:00:26.583 --> 00:00:30.382
Will have been means the auxiliary in the future perfect continuous.

8
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:32.298
I will have been working.

9
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:35.652
Your turn. Say: Will have been.

10
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:39.548
Will have been.

11
00:00:40.283 --> 00:00:41.192
Duration.

12
00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:44.810
Duration means how long something lasts.

13
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:47.184
The duration is two hours.

14
00:00:47.783 --> 00:00:50.738
Your turn. Say: Duration.

15
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.520
Duration.

16
00:00:55.283 --> 00:00:56.133
By then.

17
00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.544
By then means before or at that future time.

18
00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:01.929
By then I will have been waiting.

19
00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:04.902
Your turn. Say: By then.

20
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:08.872
By then.

21
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:10.583
By the time.

22
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:13.954
By the time means before a future event.

23
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.165
By the time you arrive, I will have been cooking.

24
00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.240
Your turn. Say: By the time.

25
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.531
By the time.

26
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:26.250
Ongoing.

27
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.435
Ongoing means still continuing.

28
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.713
An ongoing process.

29
00:01:32.317 --> 00:01:35.073
Your turn. Say: Ongoing.

30
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:39.051
Ongoing.

31
00:01:39.817 --> 00:01:41.027
Accumulated.

32
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:44.376
Accumulated means built up over time.

33
00:01:44.983 --> 00:01:46.844
Accumulated experience.

34
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:50.311
Your turn. Say: Accumulated.

35
00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:54.307
Accumulated.

36
00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.098
Milestone.

37
00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.489
Milestone means an important stage.

38
00:02:00.083 --> 00:02:01.595
A career milestone.

39
00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:04.812
Your turn. Say: Milestone.

40
00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:08.598
Milestone.

41
00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:10.432
Projection.

42
00:02:11.417 --> 00:02:14.055
Projection means a forecast of the future.

43
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:16.301
A projection of growth.

44
00:02:16.917 --> 00:02:19.523
Your turn. Say: Projection.

45
00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:23.275
Projection.

46
00:02:24.067 --> 00:02:27.567
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:28.417 --> 00:02:31.960
Visal, how long will you have been studying English by next year?

48
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:44.282
By next year I will have been studying English for ten years. The future perfect continuous looks back from a future point and stresses the duration of an ongoing activity.

49
00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:46.640
How is it formed?

50
00:02:47.750 --> 00:03:02.189
Will have been + -ing: By June she will have been teaching for twenty years. The time expression usually starts with by, by the time or in. The activity may still continue after that point.

51
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.538
How does it differ from the future perfect?

52
00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:21.960
The future perfect stresses completion: I will have finished by five. The continuous stresses duration or the activity: I will have been working all day. Stative verbs like know need the simple form.

53
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:24.914
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.750 --> 00:03:31.442
Grammar focus: Future perfect continuous: will have been plus verb-ing plus duration.

55
00:03:31.742 --> 00:03:32.882
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:33.583 --> 00:03:36.999
By next year, I will have been studying English for ten years.

57
00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:40.939
By June, she will have been teaching for twenty years.

58
00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.941
By the time you arrive, I will have been cooking for two hours.

59
00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:53.069
Will have been + -ing stresses how long an activity will have continued by a future time. Use the future perfect for completion.

60
00:03:53.817 --> 00:03:57.328
Be careful! Don't say: I will be work for ten years.

61
00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:02.044
Say: I will have been working for ten years.

62
00:04:02.344 --> 00:04:04.077
Use will have been + -ing.

63
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:07.623
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:08.433 --> 00:04:10.119
Looking Back from the Future.

65
00:04:10.419 --> 00:04:13.963
By next June, I will have been learning English for ten years.

66
00:04:14.967 --> 00:04:19.056
By the time the course ends, we will have been studying together for a year.

67
00:04:20.067 --> 00:04:23.831
The company will have been operating for a century by 2040.

68
00:04:24.833 --> 00:04:27.797
Our teacher will have been teaching for thirty years by then.

69
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.798
This tense measures duration from a future point of view.

70
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:36.913
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.400
By June she blank teaching for 20 years.

72
00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.051
A, will have been. B, will be. C, has.

73
00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.018
The answer is A: will have been.

74
00:04:51.183 --> 00:04:53.961
The future perfect continuous stresses blank.

75
00:04:54.261 --> 00:04:58.053
A, duration. B, completion. C, a habit.

76
00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:03.658
The answer is A: duration.

77
00:05:04.817 --> 00:05:07.675
By the time you arrive, I blank cooking for two hours.

78
00:05:07.975 --> 00:05:11.501
A, will have been. B, am. C, will be.

79
00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:17.333
The answer is A: will have been.

80
00:05:18.483 --> 00:05:20.645
Which verb needs the simple form?

81
00:05:20.945 --> 00:05:24.065
A, work. B, know. C, study.

82
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.460
The answer is B: know.

83
00:05:30.617 --> 00:05:34.172
According to the reading, how long will the teacher have been teaching?

84
00:05:34.472 --> 00:05:38.683
A, thirty years. B, ten years. C, a year.

85
00:05:42.233 --> 00:05:43.989
The answer is A: thirty years.

86
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.410
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:53.177
Write a sentence about how long you will have been studying English by next year.

88
00:06:01.983 --> 00:06:03.321
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:03.621 --> 00:06:14.984
By next year, I will have been studying English for six years. By then I will have been attending this school for three years. This tense shows how long the activity has lasted.

90
00:06:15.284 --> 00:06:17.469
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:18.383 --> 00:06:24.960
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and future perfect continuous: will have been plus verb-ing plus duration.

92
00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:29.526
Today's value is perseverance. Keep trying, even when it is hard.

93
00:06:29.826 --> 00:06:32.687
See you in Unit twenty-six. Goodbye!

94
00:06:32.987 --> 00:06:36.162
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

